  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nQuiet weather with warming temperatures reaching into the low \n50s, light afternoon west to SW winds of 5 to 15 MPH will continue\nfor Thanksgiving and Friday as high pressure keeps control. \nSaturday morning an upper trough will pass through the Pacific NW,\nwith the models keeping the core of this system to the north, the\nmain impact of this system will be to higher elevation winds for \nElko and Humboldt counties. Winds above 6000 feet could be strong \nwith SW winds reaching 20 to 30 MPH with gusts up to 45 MPH \npossible early Saturday morning. Timing for the strongest winds \nlook to occur between midnight an 10 AM, which may keep the \nstrongest winds from mixing down to the passes and Valleys. \nPrecipitation chances will be limited to along and north of the \nI-80 corridor, with a 10% to 20% chance for isolated showers, \nfurther north along the ID/OR border chances rise to 20% to 40% \nfor isolated to scattered showers. Snow levels will be rather high\nwith this system at about 6500 feet, which will keep any snow \nfrom roadways with the exception of NV 225 between Wildhorse Res. \nand Mountain City, with lower elevations seeing rainfall. Amounts \nwill be light with up to a 0.10\u201d of rain, and 1\u201d to 4\u201d of snow for\nthe mountain peaks along and north of I-80. Sunday will be a \nquiet day as a fast moving shortwave ridge follows in the wake of \nthe trough. Monday, Tuesday and into Wednesday will see a series \nof Pacific troughs take aim at the west, which will bring several \nrounds of widespread valley rain and mountain snow chances back to\nthe Great basin. Models are showing three troughs to this group \nwith impacts possible for each day. The first system on Monday \nlooks to be the weakest, and primarily affecting northern Nevada, \nwith isolated valley rain and mountain snow showers with light \naccumulations possible with up to 0.10\u201d of rain and 1\u201d to 3\u201d of \nsnow for the peaks. The second system will primarily affect \ncentral Nevada, and looks to be stronger than the first and \nslightly cooler, with snow levels around 5500 feet for N NV, and \n6000 feet for Central NV. So still a mix of valley rain mountain \nsnow for the end of the week, as for amounts, N NV looks to get \nbetween 0.05\u201d up to 0.25\u201d of liquid, with up to 2\u201d to 5\u201d of new \nsnow for the range peaks. Central NV looks to get between 0.10\u201d to\n0.75\u201d of water with up to 3\u201d to 8\u201d of snow for the highest passes\nand range summits. The third system models are still rather \nuncertain with as to location and timing, as it look to begin \nmoving through late Wednesday into Thursday of next week. \nTemperatures for now look to hold in the mid 40s to mid 50s for \nhighs, and with lows holding in the mid 20s to mid 30s.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence in quiet weather pattern lasting through\nFriday of this week with high pressure dominating.\n\nThere is medium confidence in a quick moving upper trough passing\nthrough the Pacific NW bringing a low 10% to 40% chance of \nisolated to scattered showers for northern Nevada Saturday.\n\nThere is medium confidence in a series of upper level troughs \nmoving through Nevada beginning Monday that will bring periods of \nrain and snow over the following few days.",
